-- Developer track, Level 3: explanations that name the wrong options by their text instead of a letter.
-- The app shuffles option order for each attempt, so "B: ..." in an explanation would point at the wrong option.
-- Updates the 75 Level 3 questions inserted by 0015, matched by question text. Safe to re-run.
with fix(question, explanation) as (values
  ($l3$A support assistant runs on `claude-opus-5-5` across 12 worker processes behind a load balancer. Each request has 14 tool definitions (about 6,000 tokens, `cache_control` on the last tool), a 30,000-token system prompt with its own breakpoint, and the conversation. After a release, almost every request, including back-to-back requests from the same user a minute apart, shows the usage below. The release made four changes. Which one, on its own, explains zero cache reads on nearly every request?

```json
"usage": {
  "input_tokens": 412,
  "cache_creation_input_tokens": 36188,
  "cache_read_input_tokens": 0,
  "output_tokens": 655
}
```$l3$, $l3$Tools render first in the prefix, so any change in tool order invalidates every cache tier. String hashing in a Python `set` is randomised per process, so each of the 12 workers can emit a different order and requests rarely land on a worker with a matching entry. “tool_choice now switches between {"type": "auto"}…”: changing `tool_choice` keeps the tools and system caches and invalidates only the messages tier, so reads would still be about 36K. “A line Current time: <ISO-8601 timestamp>…”: a timestamp in the system block breaks the system and messages tiers, but the 6K tools breakpoint would still be read. “Users can now attach screenshots, which…”: images affect only the messages tier. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching$l3$),
  ($l3$A contract-analysis job fires 40 requests at the same moment. All 40 share an identical 80,000-token prefix (system prompt plus the contract) with a `cache_control` breakpoint at its end, and each asks a different question. Every response reports the full prefix in `cache_creation_input_tokens` and zero in `cache_read_input_tokens`. Which change makes 39 of the 40 requests read the cache?$l3$, $l3$A cache entry becomes readable only after the first response begins streaming, so requests sent at the same moment all write and none read. Sending one first and fanning out after its first token turns 39 writes into reads. “Switch the breakpoint to a 1-hour…”: TTL controls how long an entry lives, not when it becomes readable. “Add a second breakpoint halfway through…”: the second breakpoint has the same timing problem. “Submit the 40 requests through the…”: cache hits inside a batch are best-effort, not a shared write. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching$l3$),
  ($l3$A latency-sensitive assistant has a 25,000-token system prompt that is identical for every user. After each deploy, the first real user request is visibly slower than the rest because it pays the cache write. What is the documented, cheapest way to remove that first-request penalty?$l3$, $l3$A `max_tokens: 0` request runs prefill, writes the cache at the breakpoint, and returns `content: []` with `stop_reason: "max_tokens"`. It bills the cache write and no output tokens. “At startup, send a request with…”: works, but generates and bills output for a throwaway answer. “At startup, call POST /v1/messages/count_tokens with…”: token counting does not write the prompt cache. “Set ttl: "1h" on the breakpoint…”: a TTL applies only after an entry is written, and deploying creates no entry. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching$l3$),
  ($l3$A research assistant uses top-level automatic caching (`cache_control` on `messages.create()`). Each user turn is one question text block followed by 24 separate text blocks of retrieved chunks. Turns arrive about 40 seconds apart, and nothing earlier in the payload changes. From the second turn on, every request shows `cache_creation_input_tokens` close to the full conversation size and `cache_read_input_tokens: 0`. What explains it?$l3$, $l3$Each breakpoint walks back at most 20 positions to find an earlier entry. The assistant reply plus 25 new blocks is about 26 positions, so the previous turn's entry is never found and the whole conversation is written again. An intermediate breakpoint (for example after the 12th chunk) fixes it. “Each retrieved chunk is shorter than…”: the minimum applies to the cached prefix as a whole, not to each block. “Automatic caching only caches the system…”: automatic caching places the breakpoint on the last cacheable block, which here is in the messages. “The 5-minute TTL expires between turns…”: 40 seconds is well within 5 minutes. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching$l3$),
  ($l3$A code-review service is moved from `claude-opus-5` to `claude-opus-5-5` by changing only the model string. The request never set `thinking` or `effort`. Latency and cost drop, but reviewers report noticeably shallower analysis on the hardest diffs. What is the most likely cause and the right fix?$l3$, $l3$Claude Opus 5.5's default effort is `medium`, one level below Claude Opus 5's `high`, which matches cheaper, faster and shallower output. Set effort explicitly. “Claude Opus 5.5 runs without thinking…”: omitting `thinking` runs adaptive thinking on Claude Opus 5.5. “Claude Opus 5.5 uses a new…”: Claude Opus 5.5 uses the same tokenizer and 1M context as Claude Opus 5. “Claude Opus 5.5 needs an explicit…”: `budget_tokens` returns a 400 on Claude Opus 5.5. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/effort$l3$),
  ($l3$This request to `claude-opus-5-5` returns `400 invalid_request_error: "thinking.type.disabled" is not supported for this model`. The team wants the lowest-latency valid request. Which change is correct?

```json
{
  "model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "max_tokens": 1024,
  "temperature": 0,
  "thinking": { "type": "disabled" },
  "messages": [{ "role": "user", "content": "Classify this ticket: ..." }]
}
```$l3$, $l3$On Claude Opus 5.5 thinking cannot be disabled at any effort level, and sampling parameters are rejected, so remove both and use `effort: "low"` for the fastest valid request. “Remove temperature and keep thinking: {"type"…”: disabled thinking still returns a 400 on its own. “Replace it with thinking: {"type": "enabled"…”: `budget_tokens` is also rejected on this model. “Replace it with thinking: {"type": "between_tools"}…”: `between_tools` is accepted only by Claude Sonnet 5.5. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/adaptive-thinking$l3$),
  ($l3$A budget guard calls `count_tokens` before every request and rejects any request estimated above 900,000 input tokens. To save money, the guard passes `model: "claude-haiku-4-5"`, but the real requests go to `claude-opus-5-5`. Requests the guard approved at about 880,000 tokens sometimes fail for exceeding the context window. Why?$l3$, $l3$Token counts depend on the model's tokenizer. The tokenizer introduced with Claude Opus 4.7, which Claude Opus 5.5 uses, can produce up to about 1.35× as many tokens as older tokenizers, so a Haiku 4.5 count undercounts. “count_tokens ignores the system prompt and…”: `count_tokens` accepts and counts `system` and `tools`. “count_tokens reports the price-adjusted count after…”: it returns raw input tokens with no price adjustment. “count_tokens is billed like a normal…”: sampling would make the guard less accurate, and it does not explain the undercount. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/token-counting$l3$),
  ($l3$A nightly job must classify 300,000 support tickets with `claude-haiku-5-5` and have every result stored before 06:00. Nobody waits on an individual result. Which design is correct?$l3$, $l3$A batch holds at most 100,000 requests (or 256 MB) and can take up to 24 hours. Results come back in any order, so join on `custom_id`, and resubmit `errored` or `expired` requests. “Submit one batch with all 300,000…”: too many requests for one batch, and the order is not guaranteed. “Send the 300,000 requests to the…”: loses the 50% batch discount and fights rate limits. “Submit one batch with all 300,000…”: one batch is still over the limit, and polling every second does not make it finish faster. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/batch-processing$l3$),
  ($l3$A 40,000-token prefix is reused by one request every 8 minutes, around the clock. It is cached with the default 5-minute TTL. Assume the standard multipliers: 5-minute cache write 1.25× base input price, 1-hour cache write 2×, cache read 0.1×. Which change gives the lowest input cost for this prefix?$l3$, $l3$With an 8-minute gap the 5-minute entry always expires, so every request pays a 1.25× write and nothing is read: about 9.4 units an hour, which is worse than no caching (7.5). With a 1-hour TTL the entry refreshes on each read, so after one 2× write each request costs 0.1×, about 0.75 units an hour. “Nothing: cache reads are 90% cheaper…”: there are never any reads. “Remove cache_control, because caching costs more…”: 7.5 units, better than now but far worse than C. “Keep the 5-minute TTL and send…”: 15 keep-alive reads plus 7.5 real reads is about 2.25 units an hour, three times the cost of C. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching$l3$),
  ($l3$A report generator calls Claude non-streaming with `max_tokens: 40000`. Behind the company's API gateway, which closes connections idle for 60 seconds, long reports fail with connection resets, while short reports succeed. Users watch a progress view while the report is written. What is the right fix?$l3$, $l3$Streaming sends events as tokens are generated, plus `ping` events, so the gateway never sees an idle connection, and the UI can render progress. Streaming is also the documented default for long outputs. “Raise the SDK's max_retries to 5…”: retries regenerate the same long report and hit the same timeout. “Move report generation to the Message…”: batches are asynchronous with no progress stream, which does not suit a user watching. “Lower max_tokens to 4,000 and ask…”: forces artificial truncation and stitching. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/streaming$l3$),
  ($l3$An agent streams responses that contain client tool calls. This handler throws `SyntaxError: Unexpected end of JSON input` on some tool calls and works on others. What is the correct fix?

```ts
for await (const event of stream) {
  if (event.type === "content_block_delta" && event.delta.type === "input_json_delta") {
    const args = JSON.parse(event.delta.partial_json);
    await dispatch(args);
  }
}
```$l3$, $l3$`input_json_delta` carries fragments of the input JSON. Only the concatenation for that block index, complete at `content_block_stop`, is valid JSON. Validate it against the schema before running the tool. “Read the tool input from content_block_start…”: in `content_block_start` the input is an empty object. “Set eager_input_streaming: false on the tool…”: buffering changes chunk size, not the fact that deltas are fragments. “Replace JSON.parse with a lenient parser…”: dispatching partial input runs tools on incomplete or truncated arguments. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/streaming$l3$),
  ($l3$A request uses the server-side web search tool. The response comes back with `stop_reason: "pause_turn"` and a content array ending in a `server_tool_use` block. What should the client do?$l3$, $l3$`pause_turn` means the server-side tool loop hit its iteration limit. Send the conversation back with the paused assistant turn as received, and the server resumes from the trailing `server_tool_use` block. “Append the assistant content, then add…”: an extra "Continue" message is unnecessary and changes the turn. “Treat it like end_turn and show…”: the turn is paused, not finished. “Send the same request again with…”: the pause is unrelated to `max_tokens`. Note that the SDK tool runners do not resume `pause_turn` automatically. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/overview$l3$),
  ($l3$A team proposes a cascade for a support assistant: every request first goes to `claude-haiku-5-5`, and low-confidence answers are re-sent to `claude-opus-5-5`. Both models would reuse one cached 50,000-token system prompt. An eval also shows `claude-opus-5-5` at `effort: "low"` matches the quality bar on all traffic. What does the documentation's guidance support?$l3$, $l3$Caches are model-scoped, so a cascade writes and pays for two cache namespaces, and every escalated request is paid twice. The guidance is to measure the most capable model at lower effort before building a cascade, and to judge cost per completed task. “Build the cascade, because the cached…”: caches are not shared across models. “Build the cascade, because cost per…”: per-request cost ignores escalations and retries. “Use the Message Batches API for…”: batches are asynchronous and add latency. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/optimizing-for-cost-and-intelligence$l3$),
  ($l3$An agent harness on `claude-opus-5-5` (account created in September 2026) adds a reminder message to the history before each request and deletes the previous reminder so only one is ever present. Thinking blocks are passed back unchanged. Requests start failing with `400 ... Invalid signature in thinking block ... bound to a different conversation`. What is the right fix?$l3$, $l3$Claude Opus 5.5 binds thinking blocks to the conversation that produced them, and accounts created on or after 2026-08-31 are checked for edited history. Deleting an earlier message is an edit, so later blocks fail validation. Keep history append-only and add instructions with mid-conversation system messages. “Strip every thinking block from the…”: stripping all thinking throws away the reasoning to work around the symptom. Stripping the named block and the ones after it is a one-time recovery, not the fix. “Set thinking: {"type": "adaptive", "display": "summarized"}…”: `display` changes visibility only, not binding. “Set thinking: {"type": "disabled"} for requests…”: disabled thinking returns a 400 on Claude Opus 5.5. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/migration-guide$l3$),
  ($l3$A gateway hard-codes a 200,000-token context window for every model and trims history to fit. After adding `claude-opus-5-5` and keeping `claude-haiku-4-5`, the team wants each model's real limits read at runtime. Which approach is correct?$l3$, $l3$Model objects from the Models API expose `max_input_tokens` (the context window) and `max_tokens` (the output cap). Claude Opus 5.5 has 1M, and Claude Haiku 4.5 has 200K. “Call GET /v1/models/{id} and read the…”: there is no `context_window` field. “Call count_tokens, which returns the tokens…”: `count_tokens` returns the input size of your request, not the remaining capacity. “Send the full history and, on…”: trial and error on 400 errors wastes requests and drops history arbitrarily. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/overview$l3$),
  ($l3$A coding assistant receives requests like "rename the `Invoice` type everywhere and update its callers". Which files need to change, and how many, can only be known after reading the codebase, and it differs a lot between requests. Which pattern from Anthropic's guidance fits best?$l3$, $l3$Orchestrator-workers fits tasks where the subtasks cannot be predicted up front; coding changes across an unknown set of files is the textbook example. “Prompt chaining: a fixed sequence of…”: chaining needs a fixed sequence. “Parallelization (sectioning): split the task into…”: sectioning needs predefined parts. “Routing: classify the request and send…”: routing picks one path; it does not decompose work. Docs: https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents$l3$),
  ($l3$Claude returns three parallel `tool_use` blocks. The client runs them and sends each `tool_result` as its own user message, so the request ends with three consecutive user messages. There are no API errors, but over long sessions Claude increasingly makes one call at a time and latency rises. What should the client do?$l3$, $l3$All results for one assistant turn belong in a single user message, with `tool_result` blocks first. Splitting them teaches Claude to stop making parallel calls. “Nothing changes the behaviour: the API…”: consecutive same-role messages are merged into one turn, so there is no error. “Send one user message that starts…”: text before the `tool_result` blocks is rejected with a 400. “Set disable_parallel_tool_use: true so the formatting…”: removes the parallelism you want for latency. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/overview$l3$),
  ($l3$An invoice extractor forced `tool_choice: {"type": "tool", "name": "record_invoice"}`. The tool is never executed: it only exists to get JSON that matches its schema. After moving to `claude-opus-5-5`, every request returns `400: tool_choice: type "tool" and "any" are not supported for this model`. Every response must still be valid JSON matching the schema. What is the right replacement?$l3$, $l3$Claude Opus 5.5 rejects forced tool use (`any` and `tool`). When the forced call only existed to extract JSON, structured outputs is the replacement and guarantees schema-valid output. “Switch to tool_choice: {"type": "any"}, since…”: `any` is also rejected. “Keep the forced tool_choice and add…”: `strict` does not lift the restriction. “Switch to tool_choice: {"type": "auto"} and…”: `auto` does not guarantee a call, so it fails the "every response" requirement without check-and-retry logic. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/structured-outputs$l3$),
  ($l3$A team uses the TypeScript SDK's `messages.parse()` with a Zod schema whose `summary` field is `z.string().max(280)`. Most responses parse, but some throw a client-side validation error because `summary` is longer than 280 characters. Why, and what is the right handling?$l3$, $l3$Structured outputs does not support string-length or numeric constraints. The Python and TypeScript SDKs strip them from the schema and validate on the client, so the model was never constrained. “Constrained decoding enforces maxLength, so this…”: `maxLength` is not enforced by decoding, so the SDK is behaving as designed. “Add strict: true to the request…”: `strict` is for tool definitions and cannot enforce unsupported keywords. “The response was cut off by…”: a parse that fails on length is not a truncation. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/structured-outputs$l3$),
  ($l3$A legal Q&A service sends contracts as `document` blocks with `citations: {"enabled": true}`. Product now wants the answer as JSON with fields `answer` and `sources`, where every source must point to a verifiable passage with its page number. Adding `output_config.format` to the request returns a 400. What should the team do?$l3$, $l3$Citations and structured outputs cannot be combined (400). Citation responses are already machine-readable: each cited block carries `cited_text`, `document_index` and a page location for PDFs, so code can assemble verifiable JSON. “Keep both features and move citations…”: there is no such top-level field, and the conflict remains. “Keep citations and prefill the assistant…”: prefilling the assistant turn returns a 400 on current models. “Turn citations off and use structured…”: model-written quotes and page numbers are not verified against the source. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/citations$l3$),
  ($l3$A long-running research agent reads large files with a tool. Old tool results now take most of the context, cost is rising, and answers are getting worse. Compliance requires the full user and assistant conversation to be kept verbatim. Only the bulky old tool outputs can go. Which feature fits?$l3$, $l3$Context editing clears old tool results (optionally their inputs) while leaving the rest of the conversation as is. “Server-side compaction, which summarises earlier context…”: compaction summarises earlier context, so the verbatim conversation is not kept in what the model sees. “The memory tool, so the agent…”: the memory tool is for persisting information across contexts; it does not shrink the current one. “A larger-context model, so old tool…”: more room does not fix cost or attention dilution. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/context-editing$l3$),
  ($l3$A RAG system splits annual reports into 400-token chunks. A query about "ACME's Q2 revenue growth" retrieves chunks like "Revenue grew 3% over the previous quarter" that never name the company or period, and wrong chunks often outrank the right ones. Which change best addresses this?$l3$, $l3$Contextual retrieval prepends chunk-specific context before embedding and BM25 indexing; Anthropic reported large drops in failed retrievals, larger still with reranking, and prompt caching keeps the per-chunk context call cheap. “Increase top-k from 20 to 100…”: more candidates add noise and tokens without fixing ranking. “Increase the chunk size to a…”: whole reports defeat retrieval and blow the token budget. “Ask Claude to generate the embedding…”: Claude does not produce embeddings; use an embedding model. Docs: https://www.anthropic.com/news/contextual-retrieval$l3$),
  ($l3$A prompt asks: "Which of these contracts allow termination for convenience?", followed by 12 contracts pasted one after another (about 120,000 tokens). Answers miss clauses and mix up which contract says what. Which restructuring follows Anthropic's long-context guidance?$l3$, $l3$Long-context guidance: put long documents at the top, the query at the end, wrap each document in tags with metadata, and ask for relevant quotes first. “Keep the question first, and add…”: a reminder does not fix query-first ordering. “Send each contract in its own…”: loses cross-contract comparison and attribution. “Summarise each contract with a cheaper…”: summaries drop the clause wording the question depends on. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/overview$l3$),
  ($l3$A `book_meeting` tool has `"date": {"type": "string", "description": "Meeting date"}`. In production Claude sometimes sends `"next Friday"` or `"14/03"`, and the booking service rejects them. The model is `claude-opus-5-5`. Which change guarantees a valid date string?$l3$, $l3$Strict tool use guarantees the input validates against the schema, and `date` is a supported string format, so the value must be a valid date. “Add "minLength": 10, "maxLength": 10 to…”: `minLength`/`maxLength` are not supported. “Add "Always use ISO 8601 dates"…”: a prompt instruction lowers the error rate but guarantees nothing. “Force the tool with tool_choice: {"type"…”: forced `tool_choice` returns a 400 on Claude Opus 5.5 and would not enforce a format. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/overview$l3$),
  ($l3$A multi-tenant assistant uses the memory tool. The client maps `/memories` to `storage/<tenant_id>/`. A security review finds this tool call in a log: `{"command": "view", "path": "/memories/../tenant_8841/notes.md"}`, and the client returned the other tenant's file. What is the correct fix?$l3$, $l3$The memory tool is client-side: your code runs each command, so it must enforce isolation by resolving paths and rejecting traversal outside the tenant root. “Nothing in code: Claude only uses…”: the log proves the model can emit such paths, and injected content can steer it. “Add "Never access other tenants' memories"…”: a prompt is not a security boundary. “Switch to the server-side memory tool…”: the memory tool runs in your application, not on Anthropic's side. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/memory-tool$l3$),
  ($l3$To stop runaway agents, a team set `max_tokens: 2000` on every turn. Now some turns end with `stop_reason: "max_tokens"` partway through a `tool_use` block, and the loop crashes parsing the truncated input. The real goal is to cap the total work of the agent run. What is the right design?$l3$, $l3$`max_tokens` is a hard per-response cap the model cannot see, so it truncates mid-tool-call. Cap the run in your loop, and use a task budget (advisory, the model sees a countdown) for the whole loop. “Raise max_tokens to 128,000 on non-streaming…”: very large non-streaming requests risk HTTP timeouts and remove all control. “Keep max_tokens: 2000 and, on max_tokens…”: re-sending a truncated turn does not resume it; the input was already cut. “Set effort: "max" so the agent…”: effort controls thinking depth; it is not a run budget. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/overview$l3$),
  ($l3$A claims app sends two photos of a damaged car (before and after repair) in one user message, followed by "Which photo shows the dented rear bumper?". Claude often names the wrong photo. Which change follows the vision guidance?$l3$, $l3$With several images, label each one ("Image 1:", "Image 2:") and place images before the text that asks about them. “Merge the two photos side by…”: merging removes the clean separation the question depends on. “Send the photos at a higher…”: resolution is not the problem. “Put the question first and the…”: question-first ordering is the opposite of the guidance. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/vision$l3$),
  ($l3$A team needs an agent that runs every night, edits files and runs shell commands in an isolated workspace, keeps a versioned configuration that each run is pinned to, and must not require the team to host the loop or the sandbox. Which surface fits?$l3$, $l3$Only Managed Agents provides both the harness and managed deployment: versioned agents, per-session containers for files and bash, and scheduled deployments. The Tool Runner, the Agent SDK and a manual loop are harness-only or less: the team would host the loop, the sandbox and the scheduler. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/managed-agents/overview$l3$),
  ($l3$A two-step chain asks for JSON with structured outputs in step 1, then parses it in step 2. With the schema unchanged, step 2 occasionally fails with a JSON parse error on long inputs. What is the most likely cause and fix?$l3$, $l3$Structured outputs guarantees schema-valid output only when generation completes; on `max_tokens` the JSON is incomplete, and on `refusal` it may not match. Always check `stop_reason` first. “The schema's compiled grammar expires after…”: the 24-hour cache affects first-request latency only. “Streaming breaks constrained decoding; switch step…”: structured outputs works with streaming. “strict: true is missing from the…”: `strict` is a tool-definition field; `output_config.format` already constrains the response. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/structured-outputs$l3$),
  ($l3$A research orchestrator gives each of 6 parallel worker subagents its entire transcript (about 150,000 tokens) plus "handle part 3". Costs are high, and workers duplicate each other's work. Which change best follows Anthropic's guidance for multi-agent systems?$l3$, $l3$Workers do best with a clear objective, output format, tools to use and task boundaries, and return condensed findings so the orchestrator's context stays clean. Vague delegation causes duplicated work. “Keep the full transcript, but tell…”: shouting does not fix missing boundaries and keeps the cost. “Replace the 6 workers with one…”: gives up the parallelism and still fills one context. “Run the workers on the larger…”: does not address cost or duplication. Docs: https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system$l3$),
  ($l3$The committed `.claude/settings.json` contains `"deny": ["Bash(git push:*)"]`. A developer adds `"allow": ["Bash(git push:*)"]` to their own `.claude/settings.local.json`, which has higher precedence than project settings. Claude Code still blocks `git push`. Why?$l3$, $l3$Rules are evaluated deny, then ask, then allow, and the first match wins, so a deny anywhere beats an allow anywhere. “Local settings have lower precedence than…”: local settings do outrank shared project settings; precedence is not the reason. “Settings changes only apply after the…”: `/clear` resets the conversation, not permissions. “git push can only be allowed…”: bypassing all permissions is not how you lift one rule. Docs: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/settings$l3$),
  ($l3$This `PreToolUse` hook (matcher `Bash`) is meant to stop `terraform apply`. In testing, Claude runs `terraform apply` anyway, and the transcript shows only a hook error notice. What is the fix?

```bash
#!/bin/bash
cmd=$(jq -r '.tool_input.command')
if echo "$cmd" | grep -q 'terraform apply'; then
  echo "Blocked: terraform apply needs a human"
  exit 1
fi
exit 0
```$l3$, $l3$In `PreToolUse`, exit code 2 blocks the call and feeds stderr back to Claude; any other non-zero code is a non-blocking error and the tool runs. “Nothing is wrong with the exit…”: exit 1 does not block. “Print the word deny on stdout…”: plain stdout is not a decision. “Move the hook to PostToolUse so…”: `PostToolUse` runs after the command already ran. Docs: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks$l3$),
  ($l3$A team's new stdio MCP server works when its functions are called from unit tests, but in Claude Code it shows as failed or disconnects right after starting. The server code has `console.log("server ready on stdio")` at startup and `console.log` debug lines in each handler. What is the most likely cause?$l3$, $l3$In the stdio transport, stdout carries only JSON-RPC messages, so any other output breaks the protocol; logs must go to stderr. “stdio servers must announce readiness on…”: there is no readiness string. “Claude Code only supports MCP servers…”: Claude Code supports stdio servers. “The server must be added with…”: scope does not affect whether a stdio server can start. Docs: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp$l3$),
  ($l3$An MCP server's `run_query` tool returns entire result sets. On large queries, Claude Code warns about the size of the tool output and the results are cut off, so Claude reasons over partial data. What is the best fix?$l3$, $l3$Claude Code limits MCP tool output and warns on large outputs. Well-designed tools paginate or filter so Claude gets what it needs; the limit can be raised with `MAX_MCP_OUTPUT_TOKENS` when necessary. “Switch to a model with a…”: the limit is Claude Code's, not the context window. “Move the server from stdio to…”: the transport does not change the limit. “Add "always ask for small pages"…”: guidance in `CLAUDE.md` does not change what the tool returns. Docs: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp$l3$),
  ($l3$A backend calls the Messages API and wants to use the company's internal MCP server, which today runs over stdio and exposes tools and a prompt template. The team plans to use the API's MCP connector. What is required?$l3$, $l3$The MCP connector reaches remote servers over HTTP, needs both `mcp_servers` and an `mcp_toolset` with the beta header, and supports tool calls only. “Put the stdio launch command in…”: the API cannot start local processes. “Declare the server in mcp_servers; the…”: resources and prompts are not exposed through the connector. “The Messages API cannot use MCP…”: the connector exists for exactly this. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/mcp-connector$l3$),
  ($l3$An MCP server for code review should let a developer explicitly start a standard review workflow, with arguments, from Claude Code's slash-command menu. Claude should not decide on its own when to start it. Which MCP primitive fits?$l3$, $l3$MCP prompts are user-controlled templates with arguments; Claude Code shows them as slash commands. “A tool, because tools are how…”: tools are model-controlled, so Claude decides when to call them. “A resource, because the review checklist…”: resources are application-controlled content, not invocable workflows. “Sampling, because the server needs to…”: sampling is a server asking the client's model for a completion. Docs: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp$l3$),
  ($l3$A `PreToolUse` hook with matcher `Write` blocks writes to `infra/`. The team later adds a filesystem MCP server called `fs`. Claude now writes to `infra/` through it and the hook never fires. What is the fix?$l3$, $l3$Hook matchers match tool names, and MCP tools are named `mcp__<server>__<tool>`; extend the regex and read the path from that tool's input schema. “Rename the MCP server to Write…”: renaming the server does not change the tool names Claude Code generates. “Move the hook to UserPromptSubmit so…”: `UserPromptSubmit` sees the prompt, not tool calls. “MCP tools cannot be matched by…”: hooks can match MCP tools. Docs: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks$l3$),
  ($l3$A developer asks Claude Code to find why a test is flaky. The investigation reads dozens of large log files, and by the time a fix is proposed the main session's context is nearly full and earlier decisions are lost. What is the best way to structure this?$l3$, $l3$Subagents have their own context window and return only their result, keeping bulky exploration out of the main thread. “Put the log files in CLAUDE.md…”: putting logs in `CLAUDE.md` fills context at start in every session. “Run /clear every few files and…”: `/clear` throws away the decisions you need. “Raise the model's effort so it…”: effort does not increase context capacity. Docs: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents$l3$),
  ($l3$A project skill `release-notes` has this front matter, and Claude never uses it when asked to "draft the changelog for v2.4". What is the most effective fix?

```yaml
# Front matter of .claude/skills/release-notes/SKILL.md
name: release-notes
description: Helps with docs.
```$l3$, $l3$At startup only each skill's name and description are loaded; Claude decides to use a skill from the description, so it must say what it does and when to use it. “Move the full skill instructions into…”: puts the body where only metadata belongs and bloats every session. “Add "Always use every skill" to…”: a blanket rule does not tell Claude which skill fits. “Rename the folder to changelog so…”: the folder name is not what triggers it. Docs: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills$l3$),
  ($l3$In a monorepo, `packages/billing/CLAUDE.md` says "Never edit generated files in `src/gen/`". A developer starts Claude Code at the repo root and immediately asks for a billing change; Claude edits `src/gen/` before reading any billing files. Why, and what is the fix?$l3$, $l3$Claude Code loads `CLAUDE.md` files from the working directory upward at start, and files in subdirectories when it reads files in those subtrees. Rules needed before that must be in a file loaded at start, for example via an `@` import. “Nested CLAUDE.md files are never read…”: nested files are read on demand. “The nested file needs a front…”: there is no such field. “Instructions in CLAUDE.md are suggestions; only…”: hooks enforce hard rules, but memory placement is the fix for this ordering issue. Docs: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory$l3$),
  ($l3$A CI job runs `claude -p "fix the lint errors"` on each PR. The job finishes successfully but makes no changes, and the logs show Claude asking for permission to edit files and run `npm run lint`. What is the right fix?$l3$, $l3$In print mode nothing answers permission prompts, so pre-approve exactly what the job needs and use structured output for the pipeline. “Run with --dangerously-skip-permissions, since CI is…”: skips every check in a job that may hold secrets, which goes against least privilege. “Add -c so the CI run…”: `-c` continues a conversation; it does not carry approvals. “Add --verbose so the permission prompts…”: verbose output answers nothing. Docs: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/headless$l3$),
  ($l3$A developer has a user-scoped MCP server named `jira` pointing to an old URL. The repo's committed `.mcp.json` defines `jira` with the new URL, and the developer has approved the project's servers. There is no local-scoped `jira`. Which definition does Claude Code use?$l3$, $l3$When the same server name exists in several scopes, Claude Code prefers local, then project, then user. With no local entry and approved project servers, the project definition wins. “The user-scoped one, because user scope…”: user scope is the lowest. “Both, and Claude picks one per…” and “Neither: duplicate names are an error…”: clashes are resolved by precedence, not shared or rejected. Docs: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp$l3$),
  ($l3$A `PreToolUse` hook on `Bash` runs `grep -q 'rm' && exit 2` on the hook's whole stdin JSON. It now blocks `npm run format` and `git log --format=oneline`. What is the right fix?$l3$, $l3$Matching the raw JSON catches `rm` inside other words and fields. Extract `tool_input.command` and match the command itself. “Change exit 2 to exit 1…”: weakens the guard instead of fixing it. “Change the hook matcher to Bash(rm:*)…”: hook matchers match tool names; `Bash(rm:*)` is permission-rule syntax. “Append || true so the hook…”: makes the hook never block anything. Docs: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks$l3$),
  ($l3$A shared HTTP MCP server gives access to HR records, and different employees may see different records. To make setup easy, the team puts one service-account token in the `Authorization` header in the committed `.mcp.json`. What is the core problem, and the right design?$l3$, $l3$A shared token makes every request the service account's, so per-user authorization is lost. Remote MCP servers in Claude Code support OAuth per user. “The token is visible to Claude…”: the header is sent by Claude Code, not shown to the model, and `CLAUDE.md` is worse. “HTTP servers cannot carry headers, so…”: HTTP servers do accept headers. “Nothing, provided the repository is private”: a private repo does not restore per-user access control. Docs: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp$l3$),
  ($l3$A nightly service must triage new issues using Claude Code's built-in Read and Grep tools, the repo's project skills, and a custom tool that calls an internal Python function in the same service. Which integration fits?$l3$, $l3$The Agent SDK is Claude Code's harness as a library, with built-in tools, and project skills load when `settingSources` includes the project; custom in-process tools are defined as an SDK MCP server. “Run claude -p as a subprocess…”: parsing free text from a subprocess is brittle. “Use the Messages API Tool Runner…”: the Tool Runner has no built-in tools and does not read the repo's skills. “Use the Messages API MCP connector…”: the API connector supports only remote HTTP servers. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/overview$l3$),
  ($l3$A team needs an automated eval for meeting summaries: there are many valid ways to write a good summary, and they want to run it on every prompt change. Which grading approach is most appropriate?$l3$, $l3$Open-ended output suits LLM-based grading with a detailed rubric, reasoning before the score, and calibration against human labels. “Exact match against one reference summary…”: exact match fails valid summaries. “ROUGE overlap against a reference summary…”: overlap metrics miss meaning and penalise good paraphrases. “The same model that wrote the…”: a vague self-rating without a rubric is unreliable and biased. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests$l3$),
  ($l3$A new system prompt scores 81.5% against the old prompt's 80.0% on a 60-case eval, from one run of each. The model's output varies between runs. A product manager wants to ship it today. What is the most defensible response?$l3$, $l3$A 1.5-point gap on 60 cases from one run is within run-to-run variation. Repeat trials, compare per case, and require the gain to hold. “Ship it: any improvement on the…”: treats noise as signal. “Ship it only if the new…”: length is not the decision criterion. “Lower the temperature to 0 for…”: sampling parameters are rejected on current models, and one run still ignores variance. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests$l3$),
  ($l3$This wrapper surrounds every call, using the Python SDK with its default retry settings. During a traffic spike, the logs show bursts of hundreds of 429s per second and the backlog grows. What is the right change?

```python
for attempt in range(5):
    try:
        return client.messages.create(**params)
    except anthropic.RateLimitError:
        continue
```$l3$, $l3$The loop retries instantly and stacks on the SDK's own retries (up to 15 calls per request), which amplifies the spike. Use one layer, wait for `retry-after`, and back off with jitter. “Raise the loop to 10 attempts…”: more attempts make the storm worse. “On a 429, switch to a…”: rate limits apply per organization or workspace, not per key. “On a 429, immediately resend the…”: changes the quality the eval approved and hides the capacity problem. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/rate-limits$l3$),
  ($l3$A streaming chat endpoint receives HTTP 200 and starts relaying text. Mid-response the stream sends `event: error` with `{"type": "overloaded_error"}`. The code ignores unknown event types and saves what it received as the assistant's turn. What is the correct handling?$l3$, $l3$Errors can arrive inside a stream after the 200 status. The turn is incomplete, so do not commit it; retry the request. “Keep the partial turn; the next…”: a partial turn in history corrupts later requests. “Treat the 200 status as success…”: the request failed. “Retry only the remaining part by…”: assistant prefill returns a 400 on current models. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/streaming$l3$),
  ($l3$A service sends a 60,000-token shared system prompt with every request and keeps hitting its input-tokens-per-minute (ITPM) limit, while output limits have plenty of room. Which change raises effective throughput the most without a limit increase?$l3$, $l3$For most current models only uncached input tokens count toward ITPM, so caching the large shared prefix multiplies effective input throughput and cuts cost. “Spread requests across more API keys…”: limits apply to the organization or workspace, not the key. “Lower max_tokens on every request”: `max_tokens` affects output, not input. “Switch to non-streaming requests, which use…”: streaming does not change input tokens. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/rate-limits$l3$),
  ($l3$An email assistant can read the user's inbox, browse arbitrary web pages, and send email. Security worries that a web page could instruct it to forward private emails to an attacker. Which single change removes this exfiltration path most reliably?$l3$, $l3$Exfiltration needs private data, untrusted content and a way to send data out. Removing or gating the outbound channel breaks the attack whatever the injection says. “Add "Never follow instructions found in…” and “Run a prompt-injection classifier on each…”: prompts and classifiers reduce risk but can be bypassed. “Use a more capable model that…”: capability is not a security boundary. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/mitigate-jailbreaks$l3$),
  ($l3$A GitHub Actions workflow calls the Claude API with a long-lived API key stored as a repository secret. Security requires that no long-lived Anthropic credentials exist in CI. Which approach meets the requirement?$l3$, $l3$Workload Identity Federation exchanges the CI platform's identity token for a short-lived Anthropic credential, so no static key exists. “Rotate the API key monthly with…” and “Store the key in a cloud…”: the key is still long-lived; rotation and storage only shorten exposure. “Give each developer their own API…”: multiplies long-lived keys and ties CI to people. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/manage-claude/wif-reference$l3$),
  ($l3$An analysis request sends five scanned PDFs as base64 `document` blocks, about 40 MB in total, and fails with HTTP 413 `request_too_large`. The analysis needs all five documents in one request. What is the right fix?$l3$, $l3$The Messages API has a 32 MB request size limit, and base64 inflates file size. Uploading through the Files API and referencing `file_id` keeps the request small. “Gzip the base64 data before putting…”: the API does not accept compressed payloads. “Split the documents into two requests…”: loses the cross-document analysis. “Lower max_tokens so the request fits…”: `max_tokens` does not change request size. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/files$l3$),
  ($l3$An agent's final answer says a customer's plan is "Basic", which is wrong. The trace shows Claude called `get_account` correctly, the tool returned `{"plan": "Basic"}` from a stale read replica, and Claude reported that faithfully. The team's first idea is to rewrite the system prompt. What does the trace show?$l3$, $l3$The trace isolates the fault: the model behaved correctly on bad input. Fix the data source and test the tool. “The model hallucinated the plan; add…”: there was no hallucination. “The model should have doubted the…”: doubles calls without fixing the data. “Raise effort so the model notices…”: effort cannot make stale data fresh. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests$l3$),
  ($l3$A security-research assistant on `claude-opus-5-5` sometimes returns HTTP 200 with `stop_reason: "refusal"` on legitimate requests, and the code crashes reading `response.content[0].text`. What is the correct handling?$l3$, $l3$A refusal is HTTP 200 with `stop_reason: "refusal"` and a `stop_details` category, so check `stop_reason` before reading content; server-side fallbacks retry declined requests on another model in the same call. “Retry the same request on the…”: retries usually decline again and cost money. “Catch the exception and show an…”: hides the problem. “Reword the system prompt so the…”: classifier declines are not controlled by the system prompt. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/migration-guide$l3$),
  ($l3$A pairwise LLM judge compares answers from two prompts. The answer shown first wins 71% of the time, even when the same answer is shown twice. Which change most directly fixes this?$l3$, $l3$Position bias is removed by evaluating both orders and counting only consistent wins. “Use a larger judge model”: larger judges still show position bias. “Ask the judge to output only…”: removing reasoning tends to make judgments worse. “Increase the number of eval cases”: more cases measure the bias more precisely but do not remove it. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests$l3$),
  ($l3$A multi-tenant RAG assistant retrieves from one shared vector index. The system prompt says "Only use documents belonging to tenant {tenant_id}". A penetration test gets another tenant's document into the answer. What is the correct fix?$l3$, $l3$Authorization belongs in code: filter retrieval by the authenticated tenant so unauthorized data never enters the context. “Repeat the tenant rule at the…” and “Ask Claude to check each retrieved…”: prompts are not access control, and an injected document can override them. “Use a separate API workspace per…”: workspaces isolate caches and limits, not your retrieval index. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/mitigate-jailbreaks$l3$),
  ($l3$A compliance assistant answers questions from policy PDFs. Auditors require every statement to be traceable to the exact source text, and unsupported statements must not appear. Which approach meets this best?$l3$, $l3$Citations give verifiable `cited_text` and locations for each claim; allowing "I don't know" and checking that every statement is cited enforces traceability. “Ask Claude to add "(source: policy)"…”: a generic label is not verifiable. “Lower the temperature to 0 so…”: sampling parameters are rejected on current models and never guaranteed faithfulness. “Run a second model to rewrite…”: rewriting does not add traceability. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/citations$l3$),
  ($l3$A user-facing endpoint has a 30-second SLA. During an incident, some calls hung for about 30 minutes before failing. The code uses the Python SDK with default settings. Why, and what is the fix?$l3$, $l3$The SDK default timeout is 10 minutes with 2 retries, and timeouts are retried, so wall-clock time can reach timeout × (retries + 1). Set both per request to fit the SLA. “The API holds requests in a…”: the client controls its own timeouts. “Streaming was off; turning it on…”: streaming helps long outputs but does not bound a hung connection by itself. “The SDK does not time out…”: the SDK does have a timeout. Docs: https://github.com/anthropics/anthropic-sdk-python$l3$),
  ($l3$A team uses a third-party MCP server. After an automatic update, one tool's description contains hidden text telling the model to include environment variables in its arguments. What best reduces this risk going forward?$l3$, $l3$Tool descriptions enter the model's context, so a malicious update is a supply-chain injection. Control which servers and versions run and review changes. “Ask Claude in the system prompt…”: prompt defences can be overridden. “Switch the server from stdio to…”: transport does not change what descriptions contain. “Increase effort so the model notices…”: effort is not a defence. Docs: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp$l3$),
  ($l3$A consultancy turns a successful contract-review agent into a reusable accelerator. Customer-specific values are already settings with defaults. Each new customer will customise prompts, clause lists and thresholds. What must ship with the template so those changes stay safe?$l3$, $l3$A template is only safely reusable if each customisation can be tested. Shipping the eval suite with thresholds makes regressions visible before go-live. “A recorded demo video of the…”: a demo proves nothing about a changed prompt. “A fixed copy of the original…”: freezing prompts defeats customisation. “A list of every model the…”: a model list is not a test. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests$l3$),
  ($l3$An AWS-based customer wants Claude billed through their AWS account and needs new API features on the day they are released. Which access path fits best?$l3$, $l3$Claude Platform on AWS is operated by Anthropic with same-day API parity and is bought through AWS. “Amazon Bedrock through the legacy InvokeModel…”: Bedrock is partner-operated and features can arrive later; `InvokeModel` is the legacy path. “The first-party Claude API with base_url…”: the first-party client cannot be pointed at AWS endpoints. “Google Cloud Vertex AI with cross-cloud…”: Vertex is Google Cloud. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/claude-platform-on-aws$l3$),
  ($l3$A regulated customer on Google Cloud requires that inference for their data happens in the EU. The prototype uses `AnthropicVertex(region="global")`. What should production use?$l3$, $l3$On Vertex, `"global"` can route anywhere; a residency requirement needs the EU multi-region or a specific EU region, with the model's availability there confirmed. “Keep "global", since it is the…”: availability does not satisfy residency. “Use a US region, since new…”: violates the requirement. “Keep "global" and add "Process this…”: a prompt cannot control where inference runs. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/claude-on-vertex-ai$l3$),
  ($l3$A Vertex AI deployment of an older dated model fails with 404 model not found. The config is shown below. What is the fix?

```python
client = AnthropicVertex(project_id="acme-prod", region="europe-west1")
client.messages.create(model="claude-opus-4-5-20251101", max_tokens=1024, messages=msgs)
```$l3$, $l3$On Vertex, dated snapshot models use an `@` separator; current-generation models use the bare ID. “Add the anthropic. prefix: anthropic.claude-opus-4-5-20251101”: the `anthropic.` prefix is Bedrock's convention. “Drop the date and use claude-opus-4-5-latest”: there is no such alias. “Switch to the first-party Anthropic() client…”: use the dedicated Vertex client, not a `base_url` override. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/claude-on-vertex-ai$l3$),
  ($l3$A team's CI runs the eval suite only when the hash of the `prompts/` directory changes. The model ID and `effort` come from environment variables in the deploy config. A release changed the model from `claude-sonnet-5` to `claude-sonnet-5-5` and went to production without an eval run, and quality dropped. What is the root fix?$l3$, $l3$The gate fingerprinted only part of what changes behaviour. Any change to model, effort, thinking, tools, schemas or prompts must trigger the eval. “Run the eval suite once a…”: lets un-evaluated changes ship between runs. “Move the model ID into a…”: patches one variable and leaves effort, tools and schemas ungated. “Pin the model alias instead of…”: an alias can move without a code change, which makes the problem worse. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests$l3$),
  ($l3$A monthly job processes 2 million documents with the same 8,000-token instruction prefix. Results are needed within 24 hours, and cost is the priority. Which plan is cheapest?$l3$, $l3$The batch discount (50%) and prompt-caching discounts can be combined; inside a batch, cache hits are best-effort, so sharing an identical prefix still helps. “Use the synchronous API with caching…” and “Use the Message Batches API without…”: batches do support caching. “Use the synchronous API with high…”: the batch discount applies at any size, and the synchronous API at this volume costs more and fights rate limits. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/batch-processing$l3$),
  ($l3$After a customer engagement, a team wants to contribute an extraction pattern they built to a public example repository. Which version is appropriate to contribute?$l3$, $l3$Contributions must be generalised, use no customer data, be cleared for IP under the engagement terms, and respect licences and attribution. “The full customer repository, so the…”: exposes customer code and IP. “The pattern with the customer's real…”: removing names does not make real contracts safe to publish. “The pattern as it is, crediting…”: naming a customer without consent creates new problems. Docs: https://github.com/anthropics/claude-cookbooks$l3$),
  ($l3$A team moves from `claude-opus-4-6` to `claude-opus-5-5`, whose per-token price is lower. A month later the cost per completed task is higher. Which explanation and response is most accurate?$l3$, $l3$The newer tokenizer (introduced with Opus 4.7) can produce up to about 1.35× the tokens of older ones, and Claude Opus 5.5 always thinks, so token counts and output tokens rise while per-token price falls. Re-baseline and judge cost per completed task. “The price list is wrong; contact…”: the price is not the issue. “Prompt caching broke because Claude Opus…”: Claude Opus 5.5's minimum cacheable length is lower than Opus 4.6's. “Claude Opus 5.5 does not support…”: batches are supported. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/migration-guide$l3$),
  ($l3$A team is ready to replace a production prompt and model configuration. It passed offline evals. Which rollout plan best limits risk?$l3$, $l3$Shadow, then canary with live monitoring and staged expansion, with the previous configuration ready, catches problems offline evals miss while limiting the blast radius. “Switch all traffic at once on…”: all at once with complaints as the alarm. “Deploy to all users, but only…”: still exposes every new conversation. “Let each user choose the new…”: hands release safety to users. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests$l3$),
  ($l3$A cost dashboard estimates each call's input cost as `usage.input_tokens × input price`. Finance finds the dashboard far below the invoice for a service that uses prompt caching heavily. What is wrong?$l3$, $l3$With caching, `input_tokens` covers only the uncached part; total input is `input_tokens + cache_creation_input_tokens + cache_read_input_tokens`, each priced differently (writes above base, reads far below). “input_tokens already includes cached tokens, so…”: they are reported separately. “The invoice includes count_tokens calls, which…”: token counting is free. “Output tokens are billed at the…”: output has its own price and the dashboard does not use it here. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching$l3$),
  ($l3$A customer's organization has Zero Data Retention (ZDR) and asks for "the most capable model available" for a legal-research agent. In testing, every request to `claude-fable-5-1` returns a 400 `invalid_request_error`, whatever the payload. What is the right next step?$l3$, $l3$Claude Fable 5.1 requires 30-day data retention and is not available under ZDR unless Anthropic expressly authorises it, which is why every request fails regardless of payload. “Add a ZDR header to each…” and “Set a per-request retention value of…”: there is no request-level switch. “Call Claude Fable 5.1 through Amazon…”: the requirement is not a platform loophole. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/migration-guide$l3$),
  ($l3$To separate teams, the same high-volume service was split across two API workspaces, each handling half of the identical traffic. Cache hit rates dropped noticeably. Why?$l3$, $l3$On the Claude API, caches are isolated per workspace, so identical prefixes in two workspaces write and read separate entries. “Workspaces have lower rate limits, so…”: limits are not the cause. “Caching is only available in the…”: caching works in every workspace. “Splitting traffic changes the model version…”: workspaces do not change model versions. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching$l3$),
  ($l3$A Python service on AWS calls Claude on Amazon Bedrock with the code below and gets authentication errors. What is the fix?

```python
client = anthropic.Anthropic(
    base_url="https://bedrock-runtime.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com",
    api_key=os.environ["AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY"],
)
```$l3$, $l3$Bedrock authenticates with AWS SigV4, which the dedicated client handles; Bedrock model IDs take the `anthropic.` prefix. “Put the AWS access key ID…”, “Add the anthropic-version header with the…” and “Move the service to us-east-1, where…”: the first-party client with a `base_url` override cannot sign AWS requests, whatever key or region is used. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/claude-on-amazon-bedrock$l3$),
  ($l3$Six months after launch, an insurance-claims assistant still passes its original eval suite, but complaints rise about new claim types introduced by a product change. What does this indicate, and what is the fix?$l3$, $l3$Passing a fixed eval while users complain about new inputs is a coverage gap: production traffic has drifted. Keep the eval set current with sampled, labelled production data. “The model has degraded over time…”: a pinned model does not degrade on its own. “The eval suite is passing, so…”: ignores real failures. “The prompt cache holds stale answers…”: prompt caching stores prompt prefixes, not answers. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests$l3$),
  ($l3$A launch on `claude-opus-5-5` expects a peak of 30 requests per second at about 700 output tokens each, about 1.26 million output tokens per minute. The organization's output-tokens-per-minute (OTPM) limit for that model is 800,000. Which plan is valid?$l3$, $l3$30 × 700 × 60 = 1.26M OTPM against an 800K limit: raise the limit, flatten peaks, shift non-urgent work to batches, and cut output. “Buy Priority Tier for claude-opus-5-5 to…”: Priority Tier does not support Claude Opus 5.5. “Spread requests across several API keys…”: limits apply to the organization or workspace, not per key. “Enable prompt caching to reduce the…”: caching reduces input tokens, not output. Docs: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/rate-limits$l3$)
)
update quiz_questions q
set explanation = fix.explanation
from fix
where q.level = 3 and q.question = fix.question;

-- Check: expect 75 Level 3 questions and 0 explanations that still start a point with a single option letter
select count(*) as level_3_questions,
       count(*) filter (where explanation ~ '(^|[ .])[A-D]: ') as letter_references_left
from quiz_questions
where level = 3;
